1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
The Tennessee.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,700
In summer, a placid river.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,480
In winter, a raging beast flooding its banks,

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,680
eroding the land, spreading disease, killing its residents.

5
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,420
The plan tamed the river with dozens of dams

6
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,320
and 200,000 workers.

7
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:21,660
The fear, technical, social, and political failure.

8
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,360
Now one of the greatest engineering feats of the

9
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:30,000
century, the Tennessee Valley Authority on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:44,001
In the early 1930s, few people had greater reason

11
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,660
to curse Mother Nature than residents of the Tennessee

12
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:50,560
River Valley.

13
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,020
Every spring brought on an onslaught from above,

14
00:00:57,021 --> 00:01:01,020
up to six feet of rain pounded the valley each year,

15
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:04,020
making it one of the wettest and flood-prone regions

16
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:05,760
in all of North America.

17
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:09,660
The Great Tennessee River was a beautiful river,

18
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,660
but it could be a deadly one.

19
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:14,080
The engineers called it a flashy river,

20
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:19,080
and that meant it could rise from a relatively mild stream

21
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:23,120
to a raging killer in a very short time.

22
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,980
Floods took a frightening human toll.

23
00:01:25,981 --> 00:01:29,320
hundreds were killed, thousands left homeless,

24
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:32,160
forced to take refuge in tents.

25
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:35,720
As a matter of fact, the first word I ever said

26
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:37,520
was water coming down.

27
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,000
There had been a little flood on the little stream

28
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,360
in front of my house.

29
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:43,640
So I didn't just speak a word.

30
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:47,160
I said water coming down.

31
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:50,300
The rains took a toll on the land as well.

32
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,740
Each year, the valley lost tons of precious topsoil,

33
00:01:53,741 --> 00:01:55,980
where cotton and corn once thrived,

34
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:00,940
the land now bore brutal scars of erosion.

35
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:04,920
This was the sorry state of life in the hills of Appalachia.

36
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:08,060
The rains kept people from ever getting ahead.

37
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:10,460
Farmers lived in shacks without electric lights

38
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:11,960
or running water.

39
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,400
There were no radios, no refrigerators,

40
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:17,000
no modern conveniences.

41
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:21,060
The tired soil kept people on the brink of starvation.

42
00:02:21,061 --> 00:02:23,960
Without a sound economy, there was little money for things

43
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:25,700
like education.

44
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:30,700
Children learn just the basics in drafty, one-room schools.

45
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,600
There was little money for adequate health care, either.

46
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,140
One third of the valley was stricken every year

47
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:41,780
by a disease unheard of in most of the United States, malaria.

48
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:43,720
In many ways, the Tennessee Valley

49
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:47,560
was 100 years behind the times and showed little promise

50
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:51,060
of ever catching up.

51
00:02:51,061 --> 00:02:58,060
Ironically, the very river that had brought decades of disaster

52
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:01,000
could also provide salvation.

53
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:05,000
Water was the valley's greatest natural asset.

54
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:08,720
If properly developed, it could provide a vital route for shipping

55
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:13,920
and enough electric power to fuel an industrial revolution.

56
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:16,760
What residents needed was the money and the know-how

57
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:19,660
to harness the river's great potential.

58
00:03:19,661 --> 00:03:24,060
They would get it in 1933 as part of President Franklin

59
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,200
Roosevelt's New Deal.

60
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:29,700
As Roosevelt took office, the entire nation

61
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:33,180
was suffering the same economic misfortunes that had befallen

62
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,040
the Tennessee Valley for generations.

63
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:39,980
The Great Depression robbed 15 million people of their jobs,

64
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:42,880
25% of the workforce.

65
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:44,880
Shanty towns popped up everywhere,

66
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,420
filled with drifters seeking employment.

67
00:03:47,421 --> 00:03:52,420
men paraded through towns to offer their labor for $1 a week.

68
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:56,160
Much of the country had simply, I think, given up.

69
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:59,440
A sort of depression psychosis develops

70
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:02,440
where you haven't found a job and you haven't found a job,

71
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:10,140
and hopelessness becomes the fundamental fact of your life.

72
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,580
The Great Depression hit the Tennessee Valley

73
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:13,980
especially hard.

74
00:04:13,981 --> 00:04:18,380
Here, entire families lived on less than $100 a year.

75
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:22,380
The South, compared with the rest of the nation,

76
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,520
was much poorer.

77
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:27,220
Everything that was bad in the nation

78
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:30,460
was compounded in the South and made even worse

79
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:33,100
in the Appalachian area.

80
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:38,480
This great nation will endure as it has endured,

81
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:42,140
will revive and will prosper.

82
00:04:42,141 --> 00:04:44,380
At his inauguration, President Roosevelt

83
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:48,420
promised quick action to rescue the country from poverty.

84
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:50,620
And he picked the bleakest spot he could find,

85
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:53,820
the Tennessee Valley, to lead the way.

86
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,880
At the river town of Muscle Shoals,

87
00:04:55,980 --> 00:05:00,220
Roosevelt proposed a wild scheme, a federal super agency

88
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:03,720
that would tame the river by building a system of giant dams.

89
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:07,560
We are here because the Muscle Shoals development

90
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,940
and the Tennessee River development as a whole

91
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:14,940
are national in their aspect and are going to be treated

92
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,540
from a national point of view.

93
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,880
Construction would provide desperately needed jobs,

94
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:24,980
and dams would ultimately generate power

95
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:28,100
to improve people's lives.

96
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,800
The Tennessee Valley Authority, the TVA,

97
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:33,960
was given unprecedented powers over a huge portion

98
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,440
of the southeastern United States.

99
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:40,440
40,000 square miles were involved, nearly all of Tennessee

100
00:05:40,441 --> 00:05:44,440
and parts of Kentucky, Virginia, North Carolina, Georgia,

101
00:05:44,441 --> 00:05:46,440
Alabama, and Mississippi.

102
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:51,440
Roosevelt included the entire Tennessee River, all 652 miles,

103
00:05:51,441 --> 00:05:53,440
as well as thousands of miles of tributaries,

104
00:05:53,441 --> 00:05:55,440
an entire watershed.

105
00:05:55,441 --> 00:06:01,440
All together then, what he saw was a system of regional planning,

106
00:06:01,441 --> 00:06:04,440
the first real attempt to do that anywhere in this country.

107
00:06:04,441 --> 00:06:07,440
And using the Tennessee Valley as a model,

108
00:06:07,441 --> 00:06:10,880
If TVA worked for the social and economic development

109
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:13,780
of that entire region, then he thought

110
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:17,020
we could replicate other TVAs around the country.

111
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:20,980
And eventually, he envisioned a national planning agency,

112
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:23,560
a national TVA.

113
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:26,020
President Roosevelt proposed the TVA

114
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:29,200
in his first 100 days in office.

115
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,960
The bill was highly controversial and extremely

116
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:35,200
vague about precisely what TVA would do.

117
00:06:35,201 --> 00:06:38,100
But the president rammed it through Congress in just five

118
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:38,900
weeks.

119
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,680
To lead his bold experiment, the president

120
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:50,280
picked two men who would become one of history's great odd

121
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:51,520
couples.

122
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:54,380
Actually, the TVA board was comprised of three members,

123
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,780
but two of the three quickly stood out

124
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:00,620
for their unbridled drive and ambition.

125
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:03,120
For chairman, the president picked America's foremost

126
00:07:03,121 --> 00:07:05,520
hydraulic engineer, Arthur Morgan.

127
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:10,220
My father was very tough-minded, but soft-hearted.

128
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,360
He had this enormous commitment to human well-being,

129
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:17,100
to humanity, to the future.

130
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,980
Morgan was the epitome of the self-made man.

131
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:24,480
He had quit college after just two months to travel.

132
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:28,840
He picked up technical skills by working as a logger, miner,

133
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:30,340
and farmhand.

134
00:07:30,341 --> 00:07:33,080
Morgan used his knowledge to build an innovative flood

135
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:38,340
control project in Ohio, which won international acclaim.

136
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:40,180
Despite his lack of formal training,

137
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:42,580
Morgan became president of Antioch College,

138
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,580
a small experimental school in Yellow Springs, Ohio.

139
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,320
He was definitely a dreamer.

140
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:55,100
But he was also extremely able in bringing his dreams to life.

141
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,340
He has been characterized as a utopian.

142
00:07:58,341 --> 00:08:03,780
But he was a down-to-earth Utopian, one who made Utopia happen.

143
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:07,980
Behind his stern expressions was a charismatic man,

144
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:11,560
one who persuaded engineers to work for the good of society.

145
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,960
It was incredible how he could create disciples

146
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:17,200
with his talk about cooperation and goodwill

147
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:21,360
and straightforwardness and the importance of always

148
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:24,640
acting in the proper way for the long run,

149
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:27,340
never being expedient for the here and now.

150
00:08:27,341 --> 00:08:30,240
On the other hand, if he didn't inspire you,

151
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:32,340
if you didn't become his disciple,

152
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:36,580
you were more than likely to find him a bit narrow-minded,

153
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:41,480
a bit dogmatic, and quite a bit paternalistic,

154
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:45,300
and find him altogether too much to take.

155
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,400
Beside Morgan on the TVA board

156
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:50,000
sat a man young enough to be his son,

157
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:53,400
a man with no engineering skills whatsoever.

158
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:57,100
David Lilienthal was a Harvard-educated lawyer.

159
00:08:57,101 --> 00:08:59,400
who was picked for his brilliant legal mind

160
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:01,740
and keen sense of politics.

161
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:04,400
Lillian Thal had forged his reputation

162
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:09,080
by attacking utility companies in Chicago and Wisconsin.

163
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:13,080
He'd won huge refunds for customers who'd been overcharged

164
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:15,360
by the phone company.

165
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:19,760
While Arthur Morgan saw TVA as a giant engineering experiment,

166
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:21,900
Lillian Thal saw something else.

167
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,100
Lillian Thal thought in terms of the battle,

168
00:09:26,101 --> 00:09:28,740
the political battle, the struggle,

169
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:32,240
and the struggle for Lilienthal's career as well.

170
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:34,780
To Lilienthal, the Tennessee Valley Authority

171
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,940
meant electric power, proving the case

172
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:41,720
that publicly owned electrical power companies

173
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:44,020
were the way to go.

174
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:46,380
The two leaders couldn't have been more different,

175
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,760
and they began to fight one another immediately.

176
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:52,300
Franz had warned Arthur Morgan to oppose

177
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:55,060
Lilienthal's appointment, but Morgan did not.

178
00:09:55,061 --> 00:09:58,660
He had an almost messianic ability to convince people

179
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:00,200
of his ideas.

180
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:03,360
And he felt that if he could sit down with them,

181
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:06,300
they could work it out.

182
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:10,500
Together, this odd couple moved to Tennessee.

183
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:13,220
They'd been given the power of kings

184
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,420
to transform the Tennessee Valley.

185
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:22,860
TVA didn't waste a single second,

186
00:10:22,861 --> 00:10:25,260
and bulldozers were on the move just months

187
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,660
after the agency was created.

188
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:30,260
5,000 local men were hired immediately

189
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:31,900
to begin clearing land.

190
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,180
Whole forests came down so that trees would never

191
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:38,080
clog the dams and reservoirs.

192
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:40,540
The logs were salvaged and turned into lumber

193
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,540
at local sawmills.

194
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:47,180
The brush was burned in giant bonfires.

195
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:50,160
Once the land was cleared, topsoil was blasted away

196
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:51,760
to expose bedrock.

197
00:10:51,761 --> 00:10:55,160
Engineers found a bounty of raw materials in the valley.

198
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:57,060
The hills were rich with limestone,

199
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:59,460
vital ingredient for concrete.

200
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:02,640
Core samples were drilled to study the suitability of the rock

201
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:04,300
for dam building.

202
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:07,140
Then hundreds of men known as powder monkeys

203
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:09,240
carefully packed the bluffs with dynamite.

204
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:16,320
Boulders as big as cars were methodically

205
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:20,120
crushed into pea-sized pebbles to make concrete.

206
00:11:20,121 --> 00:11:23,820
Early on, TVA engineers discovered their mission posed

207
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,520
a frightening technical dilemma.

208
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:28,320
To improve navigation, the valley

209
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:30,760
needed a series of load dams with locks

210
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:35,560
so that barges could glide along the river step by step.

211
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:37,740
But flood control and power generation

212
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:41,040
required high dams to trap stormwater and slowly

213
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:43,980
release it through electric turbines.

214
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:47,440
Boats could not travel past dams like these.

215
00:11:47,441 --> 00:11:57,000
This was totally new to develop a whole river in order to get the most you could out of that river.

216
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:06,880
And our job was to get the most out, first for navigation, second for flood control, and third for power.

217
00:12:06,881 --> 00:12:15,140
You could build a system to do any one of those three, or you could do two of the three.

218
00:12:15,141 --> 00:12:21,640
But to do three of them and balance them out economically,

219
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:23,380
that was a trick.

220
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:27,080
To solve the problem, TVA would have to think big.

221
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:30,180
And only an agency with power over an entire river

222
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:33,700
could do what planners were about to propose.

223
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,700
The main stem of the Tennessee River

224
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:37,960
would get low dams for navigation.

225
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:42,200
The tributaries would get 25 high dams to control flooding

226
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:44,200
and generate power.

227
00:12:44,201 --> 00:12:47,900
It was a unified approach to corral water high in the mountains

228
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:52,080
and carefully release it like a gentle wave.

229
00:12:52,180 --> 00:12:54,540
With 35 dams in all, the TVA would

230
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,880
be the largest water control system on Earth.

231
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:01,280
We had a sense of making history,

232
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:04,080
because this was the first time they

233
00:13:04,180 --> 00:13:09,720
had developed any place in the world a whole river system

234
00:13:09,721 --> 00:13:14,620
for competing purposes, navigation, flood control,

235
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:15,860
and power.

236
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:18,320
Not everyone thought it was a good idea,

237
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:20,660
especially Tennessee hill folk.

238
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,460
Well, of course, we were surprised.

239
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,960
I guess you could almost say we were shocked.

240
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:28,380
To build dams meant that land would be taken.

241
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:31,380
If land was taken and flooded, it meant that homes would be

242
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:32,680
taken.

243
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:35,480
The demands that it made on our culture

244
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:39,080
and on our way of life, on our sense of family

245
00:13:39,081 --> 00:13:43,720
and of place, this was not happy.

246
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:47,620
TVA launched an exodus of epic proportions.

247
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:51,420
15,000 families were told they'd have to move.

248
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:53,800
Their property would be flooded by the backwash

249
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:55,700
of the giant dams.

250
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:59,260
Homes were bought by the government and destroyed.

251
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:02,040
The reservoirs would also claim the heart of the valley's

252
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:03,980
agricultural community.

253
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:09,080
300,000 acres of farmland would be flooded forever.

254
00:14:09,081 --> 00:14:13,320
The project was so invasive, it would even disturb the dead.

255
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:18,320
Entire cemeteries were unearthed and moved to higher ground.

256
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:22,120
Tennessee hill folk are known for their pride and independence.

257
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:24,520
And there were fears that violence might erupt,

258
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:25,840
but it did not.

259
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:28,240
It's a little difficult for the Federals

260
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:31,440
to come down to this valley and tell people

261
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:32,980
how to live their lives.

262
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:36,080
That problem, well, it was a problem,

263
00:14:36,081 --> 00:14:39,520
was solved because the feds brought jobs.

264
00:14:39,620 --> 00:14:43,560
And that's what this valley needed above anything else.

265
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,300
In fact, people poured out of the foothills

266
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:48,760
to sign up for construction work.

267
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:51,960
TVA was hiring like no one had hired before.

268
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:54,540
Thousands of jobs for common laborers

269
00:14:54,640 --> 00:14:56,400
and skilled technicians.

270
00:14:56,500 --> 00:15:00,500
High paying work was something the valley had never known.

271
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:05,040
With TVA taking all comers, things were looking up.

272
00:15:05,041 --> 00:15:07,440
My name was William Edwards.

273
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:11,380
I lived down Cold Creek Way.

274
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:17,520
I'm working on that project they call the TVA.

275
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:23,720
The government began it when I was but a child.

276
00:15:23,820 --> 00:15:29,660
But now they are in earnest, and Tennessee's gone wild.

277
00:15:29,760 --> 00:15:33,740
As a public works project, TVA was an overnight success.

278
00:15:33,741 --> 00:15:35,880
The valley was put to work.

279
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,740
Well, it was a very interesting place

280
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:43,280
because there were hundreds of people just like me.

281
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:45,420
Engineers came from around the country

282
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:48,420
to be part of the biggest, most exciting construction

283
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:49,560
project ever.

284
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:51,260
We had jobs.

285
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:52,960
We were happy.

286
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:55,260
And we had jobs that we liked.

287
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:58,200
All up and down the valley, they

288
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:01,740
heard the glad alarm.

289
00:16:01,741 --> 00:16:04,140
The government means business.

290
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:07,980
It's working like a charm.

291
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:13,980
The government employs us short hours and certain pay.

292
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:16,380
Oh, things are up and coming.

293
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:18,080
God bless the TVA.

294
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:30,840
TVA chairman Arthur Morgan was known

295
00:16:30,841 --> 00:16:34,780
for his boundless energy, and he set a construction schedule

296
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,980
that was nothing short of frantic.

297
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:39,640
In the early days of the TVA, there

298
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:43,880
was enormous pressure to get the work underway.

299
00:16:43,980 --> 00:16:47,360
And there were thousands of people needing jobs.

300
00:16:47,460 --> 00:16:50,200
And they told him, hurry, hurry, hurry.

301
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:51,600
And hurry he did.

302
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:53,800
Dams were going up in record time.

303
00:16:53,900 --> 00:16:56,640
And at one point, 11 dams were under construction

304
00:16:56,740 --> 00:16:59,000
simultaneously.

305
00:16:59,001 --> 00:17:02,240
Work went so quickly that foundations were being built

306
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:04,580
while engineers were still deciding

307
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:07,740
how the tops of the dam should look.

308
00:17:07,840 --> 00:17:10,140
There was just so much to be done,

309
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,940
but we didn't have to know the details.

310
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:16,820
And so we could fill in the details on the drawings later

311
00:17:16,920 --> 00:17:18,820
on.

312
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:20,720
To support this building frenzy,

313
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:24,220
huge concrete mixers were designed to churn out six tons

314
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:27,000
of cement every minute.

315
00:17:27,001 --> 00:17:29,460
An ingenious railroad and cable system

316
00:17:29,560 --> 00:17:34,240
delivered this concrete in drop bottom buckets.

317
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:37,180
Crews with pneumatic vibrators shook air bubbles out

318
00:17:37,280 --> 00:17:40,580
of the cement as it settled.

319
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:46,480
TVA's first dam, Norris, required 166,000 buckets of cement,

320
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:49,560
more than a million cubic yards, delivered around the clock,

321
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:54,160
bucket by bucket, every 90 seconds.

322
00:17:54,161 --> 00:17:59,200
When complete, Norris would be 265 feet high and 1,860 feet

323
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:00,800
across.

324
00:18:00,900 --> 00:18:04,000
It could hold an entire year's rainfall in a lake,

325
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:08,000
measuring 83 square miles.

326
00:18:08,100 --> 00:18:11,380
Giant pipes called penstocks would funnel this water

327
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:14,120
toward two electric generators.

328
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:17,580
Each section of penstock weighed 40 tons,

329
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:21,920
and moving them into place was treacherous business.

330
00:18:21,921 --> 00:18:26,400
Thousands of men risked their lives every day at TVA dams.

331
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,020
The work was gritty, backbreaking, and above all,

332
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:30,200
dangerous.

333
00:18:30,300 --> 00:18:31,960
Something like this, where you've

334
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:36,400
got these big buckets of concrete moving overhead

335
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,040
on the cableway.

336
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:40,980
If something went wrong with that cableway,

337
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:42,580
well, that bucket might fall.

338
00:18:42,680 --> 00:18:46,380
And no telling how many people would be killed below.

339
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:51,120
There were accidents, and 112 men lost their lives.

340
00:18:51,121 --> 00:18:54,420
But that was considered a remarkably good record

341
00:18:54,520 --> 00:18:56,560
for a project so big.

342
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:59,060
TVA workers received special training.

343
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:02,860
This attention to safety paid off.

344
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:05,360
In just one year, the Tennessee Valley Authority

345
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:07,740
was off to a remarkable start.

346
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:12,380
The hills were buzzing with the sounds of progress.

347
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:15,920
Arthur Morgan succeeded beyond anyone's expectations.

348
00:19:16,020 --> 00:19:17,840
And with construction well underway,

349
00:19:17,841 --> 00:19:22,440
Morgan would take the TVA into even more challenging territory,

350
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:23,940
social engineering.

351
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:33,960
The 1930s was a golden age for heavy construction

352
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:35,920
throughout the United States.

353
00:19:36,020 --> 00:19:39,120
President Roosevelt viewed dams as a surefire way

354
00:19:39,220 --> 00:19:43,340
to provide jobs and rebuild America's battered economy.

355
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:47,040
Dams were going up everywhere.

356
00:19:47,041 --> 00:19:49,840
In a steep desert gorge in southern Nevada,

357
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:52,640
the towering Hoover Dam was rapidly rising.

358
00:19:52,740 --> 00:19:57,780
It would become America's tallest dam, measuring 726 feet.

359
00:19:57,880 --> 00:19:59,780
And in the Pacific Northwest, work

360
00:19:59,880 --> 00:20:01,780
was underway on the granddaddy of dams,

361
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:06,400
the massive Grand Coulee project on the Columbia River,

362
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:09,200
a testament to those determined to make nature work

363
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:12,140
for the good of man.

364
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:15,500
Hoover and Grand Coulee were built in isolated locations

365
00:20:15,501 --> 00:20:18,740
where men endured a rugged existence in temporary work

366
00:20:18,840 --> 00:20:19,940
camps.

367
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:23,700
These tar paper towns featured raucous saloons

368
00:20:23,800 --> 00:20:26,940
and houses of prostitution.

369
00:20:27,040 --> 00:20:29,340
Back in the Tennessee Valley, Arthur Morgan

370
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:32,080
wanted his projects to be something more.

371
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:35,760
And so TVA built the town of Norris, Tennessee,

372
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:39,160
five miles downstream from Norris Dam.

373
00:20:39,260 --> 00:20:41,760
Norris would be a model community,

374
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:45,500
a vision of what life could be with proper planning.

375
00:20:45,501 --> 00:20:50,000
Morgan was quite right that you don't have to let your

376
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:53,900
construction villages turn into the hell holes that were so

377
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,240
typical of large construction projects.

378
00:20:56,340 --> 00:21:01,540
You create proper homes for your workers with families,

379
00:21:01,640 --> 00:21:03,960
proper dormitories for those who are unmarried.

380
00:21:04,060 --> 00:21:08,260
These small electrified cottages came also with a five-acre

381
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:11,060
plot for farming.

382
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:13,800
There was nothing that he wouldn't try to improve

383
00:21:13,801 --> 00:21:16,840
to improve the lives of his workers.

384
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:21,500
Norris was a family town with churches and schools.

385
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:26,380
Workers could rent a new four-room home for $14 a month,

386
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:28,180
about one week's pay.

387
00:21:28,280 --> 00:21:33,320
$5 more got you a furnace, stove, refrigerator, and water heater.

388
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:37,360
This was Arthur Morgan's dream, an idyllic community,

389
00:21:37,460 --> 00:21:40,060
a town that he himself called home.

390
00:21:40,160 --> 00:21:43,400
He even ate in the village cafeteria.

391
00:21:43,401 --> 00:21:46,280
Norris was a place where common laborers could picnic

392
00:21:46,281 --> 00:21:47,280
in the village green.

393
00:22:12,380 --> 00:22:15,780
David Lilienthal ridiculed Morgan's social programs

394
00:22:15,781 --> 00:22:19,480
programs and said valley life would improve if TVA focused

395
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:23,020
on rapidly providing cheap electric power.

396
00:22:23,120 --> 00:22:25,120
But President Roosevelt believed government

397
00:22:25,220 --> 00:22:28,160
could be a benevolent force in people's lives,

398
00:22:28,260 --> 00:22:30,920
and he wanted Arthur Morgan to prove it in the Tennessee

399
00:22:31,020 --> 00:22:32,020
Valley.

400
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:35,440
Roosevelt said to him, the TVA should relate to every area

401
00:22:35,540 --> 00:22:36,940
of human concern.

402
00:22:37,040 --> 00:22:39,780
They weren't interested just in dams, just in flood control,

403
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:41,140
just in power.

404
00:22:41,240 --> 00:22:45,080
They were interested in revitalizing the whole area

405
00:22:45,081 --> 00:22:47,580
from top to bottom.

406
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:50,720
Morgan applied his vision beyond the town of Norris.

407
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:53,280
To combat the widespread problem of illiteracy,

408
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:55,360
Morgan built a horse-drawn bookmobile

409
00:22:55,460 --> 00:22:58,860
to travel throughout the valley.

410
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:00,760
Woodworking and machine shop classes

411
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:03,640
taught people industrial skills.

412
00:23:03,740 --> 00:23:06,260
Children were vaccinated against disease.

413
00:23:06,360 --> 00:23:09,880
And to combat malaria, TVA spread enough insecticide

414
00:23:09,980 --> 00:23:14,080
to kill 1 billion malaria-breeding mosquitoes.

415
00:23:14,081 --> 00:23:16,480
To ensure that mosquitoes did not return,

416
00:23:16,580 --> 00:23:19,680
entire swamps were drained.

417
00:23:19,780 --> 00:23:23,620
Pumps sucked 100,000 gallons of murky swamp water every minute,

418
00:23:23,720 --> 00:23:26,520
sending it into the reservoirs where it could do no harm.

419
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:32,600
TVA had given the valley the tools it desperately needed.

420
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:36,300
Residents were building more than an ambitious series of dams.

421
00:23:36,400 --> 00:23:38,600
They were building a better life.

422
00:23:38,700 --> 00:23:41,800
When you consider all of the workers needed

423
00:23:41,801 --> 00:23:44,840
for this massive construction project.

424
00:23:44,841 --> 00:23:48,000
A common laborer making 45 cents an hour,

425
00:23:48,001 --> 00:23:53,640
eating all he could eat in the TVA dining hall for 25 cents,

426
00:23:53,641 --> 00:23:56,780
renting one of those electrified modern cottages

427
00:23:56,781 --> 00:23:58,820
for $14 a month.

428
00:23:58,821 --> 00:24:01,160
Boy, was that a change.

429
00:24:01,161 --> 00:24:03,820
TVA employees enjoyed the most progressive

430
00:24:03,821 --> 00:24:06,000
working conditions in the nation.

431
00:24:06,001 --> 00:24:08,960
They labored only five hours a day.

432
00:24:08,961 --> 00:24:11,120
That way, more shifts were possible,

433
00:24:11,220 --> 00:24:13,540
and everyone could get a job.

434
00:24:13,640 --> 00:24:17,060
There was work for women, good work, at drafting tables

435
00:24:17,160 --> 00:24:18,700
and in power plants.

436
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:22,300
There was also an early form of affirmative action.

437
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:25,080
Blacks were employed in proportion to their population

438
00:24:25,180 --> 00:24:26,240
in the valley.

439
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:28,740
They earned the same wages as whites,

440
00:24:28,840 --> 00:24:32,920
although they lived and sometimes worked in segregation.

441
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:36,920
Morgan was attacked by critics for using the people of Tennessee

442
00:24:36,921 --> 00:24:40,220
as guinea pigs in a giant social experiment.

443
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:43,460
But on the whole, folks in the valley were smiling.

444
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,780
The Tennessee Valley was largely a rural region

445
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:58,380
of tenant farmers who eked out a living on 70-acre plots

446
00:24:58,480 --> 00:24:59,740
of land.

447
00:24:59,840 --> 00:25:02,780
If TVA was going to make a major improvement

448
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:04,840
in the standard of living, it would

449
00:25:04,841 --> 00:25:08,040
It would have to improve life on the farm.

450
00:25:08,041 --> 00:25:10,780
As well, the devastating floods and erosion

451
00:25:10,781 --> 00:25:14,080
were actually made worse by the way farmers traditionally

452
00:25:14,081 --> 00:25:16,180
plowed their land.

453
00:25:16,181 --> 00:25:21,060
TVA offered special assistance to 23,000 people who signed up

454
00:25:21,061 --> 00:25:24,400
to be test demonstration farmers.

455
00:25:24,401 --> 00:25:26,560
The best specialists in the country

456
00:25:26,561 --> 00:25:30,740
turned these farms into living laboratories.

457
00:25:30,741 --> 00:25:34,560
First, farmers learned to plow along the natural curves

458
00:25:34,561 --> 00:25:35,560
of the land.

459
00:25:35,561 --> 00:25:38,940
Contour plowing prevents rain from racing downhill

460
00:25:38,941 --> 00:25:41,260
and causing a flood.

461
00:25:41,261 --> 00:25:44,800
Next, farmers learned to use phosphate fertilizer, which

462
00:25:44,801 --> 00:25:49,400
restored productivity to the tired soil.

463
00:25:49,401 --> 00:25:52,140
Then a broad mixture of crops was introduced,

464
00:25:52,141 --> 00:25:55,540
including clover, tobacco, and wheat.

465
00:25:55,541 --> 00:26:00,880
Community threshers improved the efficiency of the harvest.

466
00:26:00,881 --> 00:26:02,480
The plan worked.

467
00:26:02,481 --> 00:26:04,640
The demonstration farmers dramatically

468
00:26:04,641 --> 00:26:06,520
increased their harvests.

469
00:26:06,521 --> 00:26:08,220
Even though half the valley's farmland

470
00:26:08,221 --> 00:26:11,520
had been flooded by reservoirs, the total farm output

471
00:26:11,521 --> 00:26:15,660
increased by nearly 100%.

472
00:26:15,661 --> 00:26:20,440
TVA had dramatically improved life in the valley.

473
00:26:20,441 --> 00:26:23,200
But the biggest improvement was yet to come.

474
00:26:23,201 --> 00:26:26,480
Electric power would change things in ways no one

475
00:26:26,481 --> 00:26:28,040
could anticipate.

476
00:26:28,041 --> 00:26:32,040
And it would create a power struggle that would tear TVA

477
00:26:32,041 --> 00:26:35,940
apart.

478
00:26:36,040 --> 00:26:39,480
By the late 1930s, America was back on its feet.

479
00:26:39,580 --> 00:26:41,680
The Great Depression was over.

480
00:26:41,780 --> 00:26:44,380
People were busy at work once again.

481
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:47,920
The first of TVA's dams was rapidly coming online,

482
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:51,700
and the Tennessee Valley, once a backward and forgotten place,

483
00:26:51,800 --> 00:26:54,000
was suddenly hopping.

484
00:26:54,100 --> 00:26:59,200
What made the difference more than anything was power.

485
00:26:59,201 --> 00:27:03,640
In peace and war, the dams work for the people.

486
00:27:03,641 --> 00:27:05,380
Power for the factories.

487
00:27:05,381 --> 00:27:07,300
Power for new industry.

488
00:27:07,301 --> 00:27:11,780
Power to run a million machines, turning out aircraft,

489
00:27:11,781 --> 00:27:16,720
tractors, textiles, engines, shoes,

490
00:27:16,721 --> 00:27:19,920
fertilizer, aluminum.

491
00:27:19,921 --> 00:27:23,720
Cheap and abundant power to light the cities and villages.

492
00:27:23,721 --> 00:27:25,900
Power for the farmers.

493
00:27:25,901 --> 00:27:29,440
that can be converted to 100 homely uses.

494
00:27:29,540 --> 00:27:34,840
Power working tirelessly, endlessly, raising standards,

495
00:27:34,940 --> 00:27:36,940
reducing drudgery.

496
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:39,440
Power in the hands of the people.

497
00:27:45,060 --> 00:27:48,320
Power was the focus of TVA's youngest board member,

498
00:27:48,420 --> 00:27:50,460
David Lilienthal.

499
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:53,000
To ensure continued prosperity for the Valley,

500
00:27:53,001 --> 00:27:56,480
he pushed TVA to become the nation's premier public power

501
00:27:56,580 --> 00:27:58,380
company.

502
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:02,280
Lilienthal made TVA an electric dynamo.

503
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:05,680
Dams produce 12 billion kilowatt hours each year,

504
00:28:05,780 --> 00:28:10,620
enough electricity to light more than 6 million homes.

505
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:14,180
TVA sold its power at bargain basement rates.

506
00:28:14,280 --> 00:28:17,800
That was Lilienthal's great contribution.

507
00:28:17,900 --> 00:28:20,860
He's the one who understood that if you

508
00:28:20,861 --> 00:28:26,260
If you had low electric rates and if you had cheap electrical

509
00:28:26,261 --> 00:28:30,740
appliances, you could run power lines out into the countrysides

510
00:28:30,741 --> 00:28:35,840
and demand would warrant, would justify the whole process.

511
00:28:35,841 --> 00:28:40,440
TVA strung enough wire to span the nation seven times.

512
00:28:40,441 --> 00:28:43,080
Towns that had never known electric light would soon

513
00:28:43,081 --> 00:28:46,520
leave the Dark Ages behind.

514
00:28:46,521 --> 00:28:49,160
The Tennessee Valley Authority is pushing lines

515
00:28:49,161 --> 00:28:51,300
into rural communities.

516
00:28:51,301 --> 00:28:53,940
Now the cross arms swings into place,

517
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:56,740
and the insulator is securely fastened.

518
00:28:56,741 --> 00:28:59,580
The line must be tied in firmly so that high winds

519
00:28:59,581 --> 00:29:02,300
and winter storms may not interrupt the delivery of cheap

520
00:29:02,301 --> 00:29:05,300
TVA kilowatts to farms and homes.

521
00:29:05,301 --> 00:29:09,340
TVA films chronicled how drastically life was changing.

522
00:29:09,341 --> 00:29:12,220
No longer would people scrub their laundry by hand.

523
00:29:12,221 --> 00:29:19,220
The wood-burning stove gave way to the electric range.

524
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:26,560
Things we take for granted today were a godsend then.

525
00:29:35,680 --> 00:29:38,680
The valley set national records for the sale of stoves

526
00:29:38,780 --> 00:29:40,040
and refrigerators.

527
00:29:40,041 --> 00:29:45,780
Classes taught people how to use their new appliances.

528
00:29:45,880 --> 00:29:48,480
Power brought the valley into the modern age.

529
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:50,380
It even touched people's hearts.

530
00:29:50,480 --> 00:29:53,080
They could now listen to radio.

531
00:29:53,180 --> 00:29:55,580
Not for a moment have I doubted

532
00:29:55,680 --> 00:29:59,900
that we would climb out of the Valley of Glom.

533
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
To be able to hear Franklin Roosevelt,

534
00:30:02,100 --> 00:30:05,800
the man who had had this idea, to be able to hear him make

535
00:30:05,900 --> 00:30:08,840
one of his great addresses over the radio,

536
00:30:08,841 --> 00:30:12,040
This, I think, really was one of the great things.

537
00:30:12,140 --> 00:30:17,780
The radio was there and put us in touch with the world.

538
00:30:17,880 --> 00:30:22,380
TVA's system of dams worked like no single dam could.

539
00:30:22,480 --> 00:30:25,420
Storm water captured by high dams in the mountains

540
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:29,820
was carefully dispatched down the river valley.

541
00:30:29,920 --> 00:30:31,800
At every dam down the line, the river

542
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:33,900
whirled through electric turbines,

543
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,100
putting water to work again and again.

544
00:30:38,840 --> 00:30:40,980
With nature's bounty of near limitless power

545
00:30:41,080 --> 00:30:43,780
came an explosion of industry.

546
00:30:43,880 --> 00:30:45,480
Grain mills opened.

547
00:30:45,580 --> 00:30:48,680
So did a modern cheese plant and dairy.

548
00:30:48,780 --> 00:30:55,080
One factory hatched chicks to launch a local poultry industry.

549
00:30:55,180 --> 00:30:57,320
Along the river, navigation locks

550
00:30:57,420 --> 00:31:00,500
made it easy for merchants to ship their goods to market.

551
00:31:03,340 --> 00:31:05,960
A barge would enter the lock through a giant doorway

552
00:31:05,961 --> 00:31:12,500
into a chamber 110 feet wide and 600 feet long.

553
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:15,440
The doors would close, and gravity-powered pumps

554
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:19,500
would adjust the water level.

555
00:31:19,600 --> 00:31:22,080
Locks work like a giant elevator,

556
00:31:22,180 --> 00:31:27,140
gracefully raising or lowering boats to get past a dam.

557
00:31:27,240 --> 00:31:30,680
In the Tennessee Valley, locks were essential to help boats

558
00:31:30,780 --> 00:31:34,160
navigate the 513-foot elevation change

559
00:31:34,161 --> 00:31:37,600
along the run of the river.

560
00:31:37,700 --> 00:31:40,600
This steep elevation drop made the river

561
00:31:40,700 --> 00:31:43,800
a treacherous string of shoals and rapids.

562
00:31:43,900 --> 00:31:46,400
But TVA dams smoothed out the ride,

563
00:31:46,500 --> 00:31:51,720
turning the river into a series of easy steps.

564
00:31:51,820 --> 00:31:54,020
Locks move barges from step to step,

565
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:57,860
changing elevations an average of 53 feet at every dam

566
00:31:57,960 --> 00:31:58,660
along the way.

567
00:31:58,661 --> 00:32:01,560
way.

568
00:32:01,660 --> 00:32:05,400
TVA dams were true engineering marvels.

569
00:32:05,500 --> 00:32:07,140
For Valley residents, they instilled

570
00:32:07,240 --> 00:32:10,280
a sense of pride, accomplishment, and optimism

571
00:32:10,380 --> 00:32:11,280
toward the future.

572
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:21,380
Before TVA brought plentiful power to the Tennessee Valley,

573
00:32:21,480 --> 00:32:24,260
people who had electricity were at the mercy

574
00:32:24,360 --> 00:32:27,660
of private power monopolies.

575
00:32:27,661 --> 00:32:30,200
These companies offered no rural service,

576
00:32:30,300 --> 00:32:33,200
and in large towns, the price was more than most anyone

577
00:32:33,300 --> 00:32:35,440
could afford.

578
00:32:35,540 --> 00:32:38,340
The situation was similar in much of the nation.

579
00:32:38,440 --> 00:32:40,880
President Roosevelt claimed high-priced power

580
00:32:40,980 --> 00:32:43,480
was strangling the economy.

581
00:32:43,580 --> 00:32:45,340
I believe that the individual should have

582
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:48,520
full liberty of action to make the most of himself.

583
00:32:48,620 --> 00:32:52,120
But I do not believe that in the name of that sacred word,

584
00:32:52,220 --> 00:32:54,760
a few powerful interests should be

585
00:32:54,761 --> 00:32:57,860
to make industrial cannon fodder of the lives

586
00:32:57,960 --> 00:33:01,700
of half the population of the United States.

587
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:04,800
In the Tennessee Valley, industrialist Wendell

588
00:33:04,900 --> 00:33:09,980
Wilkie was a virtual power baron until TVA came along.

589
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:13,340
TVA sold power at half the price Wilkie was charging,

590
00:33:13,440 --> 00:33:16,880
which prompted Wilkie to take TVA to court.

591
00:33:16,980 --> 00:33:19,160
It would become a national test case

592
00:33:19,260 --> 00:33:22,420
on the legality of public power production.

593
00:33:22,421 --> 00:33:26,520
And if it is constitutional for the federal government

594
00:33:26,620 --> 00:33:30,400
to do that, then there is no way of stopping

595
00:33:30,500 --> 00:33:35,360
the government's invasion of every sphere of private

596
00:33:35,460 --> 00:33:38,800
enterprise, whether great or small.

597
00:33:38,900 --> 00:33:42,400
Inside the TVA, Arthur Morgan and David Lilienthal

598
00:33:42,500 --> 00:33:45,940
locked horns in a bitter feud over how to respond.

599
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:50,120
The chief problem was over two areas extremely important

600
00:33:50,220 --> 00:33:51,180
to Arthur Morgan.

601
00:33:51,181 --> 00:33:55,780
One was that you never compete, you always cooperate.

602
00:33:55,880 --> 00:33:58,420
Lillienthal, with his experience in dealing

603
00:33:58,520 --> 00:34:00,720
with the private power companies,

604
00:34:00,820 --> 00:34:03,920
knew that this was a bitter street fight

605
00:34:04,020 --> 00:34:06,700
and cooperation was going to get you nothing but a knife

606
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:07,760
in the neck.

607
00:34:07,860 --> 00:34:09,900
Morgan felt private companies should

608
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:11,800
keep their share of the market.

609
00:34:11,900 --> 00:34:14,800
TVA should serve new customers only.

610
00:34:14,900 --> 00:34:17,340
To Morgan, that seemed efficient and fair.

611
00:34:17,440 --> 00:34:19,880
But Lillienthal called this a sellout.

612
00:34:19,881 --> 00:34:22,520
The charge stung Morgan deeply.

613
00:34:22,620 --> 00:34:25,020
My father never sold out to anybody,

614
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:27,820
let alone the power coming.

615
00:34:27,920 --> 00:34:28,820
He was not.

616
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:30,420
He was never for sale.

617
00:34:30,520 --> 00:34:32,320
The internal fight turned public

618
00:34:32,420 --> 00:34:34,620
as Wendell Wilkie's case against public power

619
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:37,540
pressed through the courts.

620
00:34:37,640 --> 00:34:41,340
It seemed the pressure was too much for Arthur Morgan to bear.

621
00:34:41,440 --> 00:34:44,580
In two magazine articles, Morgan accused David Lilienthal

622
00:34:44,680 --> 00:34:47,440
of dishonest behavior at the TVA.

623
00:34:47,441 --> 00:34:50,340
Morgan said Congress should investigate.

624
00:34:50,440 --> 00:34:52,740
The charge sparked a national scandal.

625
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:54,680
President Roosevelt ordered Morgan

626
00:34:54,780 --> 00:34:57,780
to prove his allegations at an event unparalleled

627
00:34:57,880 --> 00:35:02,180
in American history, a presidential court of inquisition.

628
00:35:02,280 --> 00:35:04,060
The president of the United States

629
00:35:04,160 --> 00:35:08,160
is the judge and the prosecutor and the jury in this case.

630
00:35:08,260 --> 00:35:11,600
And the press is waiting outside getting transcripts

631
00:35:11,700 --> 00:35:12,700
of every page.

632
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:15,500
Starts on March 11, 1938.

633
00:35:15,501 --> 00:35:17,500
Morgan says, I'll have nothing to do with this.

634
00:35:17,501 --> 00:35:20,500
I've been calling for a congressional investigation.

635
00:35:20,501 --> 00:35:21,500
And then he says, I'm not prepared.

636
00:35:21,501 --> 00:35:23,500
You didn't tell me to bring any stuff.

637
00:35:23,501 --> 00:35:25,500
Roosevelt gives him a week.

638
00:35:25,501 --> 00:35:27,500
Morgan comes back and says, this is improper.

639
00:35:27,501 --> 00:35:29,500
We need a congressional investigation.

640
00:35:29,501 --> 00:35:33,500
Roosevelt says, I'll give you until Monday, and that's it.

641
00:35:33,501 --> 00:35:35,500
Morgan comes in on Monday and says, Mr. President,

642
00:35:35,501 --> 00:35:38,500
I can have nothing more to do with these affairs,

643
00:35:38,501 --> 00:35:40,500
gets on a train, leaves D.C.,

644
00:35:40,501 --> 00:35:43,500
and goes back to Yellow Springs, Ohio.

645
00:35:43,501 --> 00:35:47,900
The next day, March 22, 1938, Roosevelt fires Arthur Morgan,

646
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:51,240
thus ending in rather dramatic fashion what's clearly

647
00:35:51,340 --> 00:35:54,440
Roosevelt's toughest personnel problem.

648
00:35:54,540 --> 00:35:57,440
The visionary who had launched the greatest dam building

649
00:35:57,540 --> 00:36:01,220
project in history fell from grace amid the rough and tumble

650
00:36:01,320 --> 00:36:03,420
of politics.

651
00:36:03,520 --> 00:36:05,420
TVA was far from finished.

652
00:36:05,520 --> 00:36:08,320
Only eight of 35 dams were complete,

653
00:36:08,420 --> 00:36:11,120
and Morgan's dream of social and economic planning

654
00:36:11,220 --> 00:36:12,860
was badly bruised.

655
00:36:12,861 --> 00:36:14,700
The TVA had something started here,

656
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:17,460
which was unique in the world.

657
00:36:17,560 --> 00:36:23,500
And this is something that could have become a model.

658
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:27,940
It could have been reproduced all over the place.

659
00:36:28,040 --> 00:36:29,040
But it never was.

660
00:36:29,140 --> 00:36:31,320
There's only one TVA.

661
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:33,020
And that's a great tragedy.

662
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:35,720
It only stopped here.

663
00:36:35,820 --> 00:36:38,020
Arthur Morgan got his congressional hearing,

664
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:39,660
but nothing came of it.

665
00:36:39,661 --> 00:36:43,960
He returned to Ohio to run a community service foundation.

666
00:36:44,060 --> 00:36:48,140
David Lilienthal became TVA chairman.

667
00:36:48,240 --> 00:36:51,200
In court, Wendell Wilkie lost his battle with the TVA

668
00:36:51,300 --> 00:36:53,840
and quickly sold his power empire to the government

669
00:36:53,940 --> 00:36:57,640
for $79 million.

670
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:00,980
It made TVA the nation's largest power company.

671
00:37:01,080 --> 00:37:03,380
Still, the future looked grim.

672
00:37:03,480 --> 00:37:05,160
The TVA had survived.

673
00:37:05,260 --> 00:37:08,320
It, however, had lost much of its momentum.

674
00:37:08,321 --> 00:37:13,220
Congressional support after all of those embarrassing fights was no longer what it was.

675
00:37:13,340 --> 00:37:19,740
And the support it had enjoyed in the valley had lessened as well because of the terrible bitterness

676
00:37:19,741 --> 00:37:23,460
and this awful bloodletting at the top of its leadership.

677
00:37:24,220 --> 00:37:28,220
So far, TVA had accomplished an engineering miracle.

678
00:37:28,660 --> 00:37:33,160
The question was, could it survive long enough to finish the job?

679
00:37:38,320 --> 00:37:41,620
December 7, 1941, Pearl Harbor is attacked

680
00:37:41,720 --> 00:37:43,720
and America goes to war.

681
00:37:43,820 --> 00:37:47,060
America's entry into World War II brought new life

682
00:37:47,160 --> 00:37:49,460
to the Tennessee Valley Authority.

683
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:52,800
Dam construction had languished after political dogfights

684
00:37:52,900 --> 00:37:54,900
between TVA's leaders.

685
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:59,580
But now TVA was back on track with an added sense of urgency.

686
00:38:02,620 --> 00:38:04,780
America prepares.

687
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:07,320
Mighty rivers harnessed by towering dams

688
00:38:07,321 --> 00:38:09,960
to the service of peacetime industry,

689
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:13,260
turn their power to the nation's defense.

690
00:38:13,360 --> 00:38:16,300
Here in the Tennessee Valley is a tremendous power plant,

691
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:19,300
a source of natural energy that is forging modern armor

692
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:21,260
for Uncle Sam.

693
00:38:21,360 --> 00:38:25,340
To provide even more power, TVA built dams quicker

694
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:28,040
than they'd ever been built before.

695
00:38:28,140 --> 00:38:32,040
Douglas Dam, 200 feet high and 1,700 feet across,

696
00:38:32,140 --> 00:38:35,820
went up in just 12 months.

697
00:38:35,821 --> 00:38:41,120
Government posters stressed how important dam construction was.

698
00:38:41,220 --> 00:38:43,360
Throughout the valley, peacetime production

699
00:38:43,460 --> 00:38:48,360
was converted to manufacturing the material of war.

700
00:38:48,460 --> 00:38:51,860
Fertilizer factories retooled to make ammonium nitrate

701
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:55,480
essential for bombs, grenades, and bullets.

702
00:38:55,580 --> 00:39:00,780
The valley turned out 300 tons of ammonium nitrate every day.

703
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:03,480
Textile mills made hats and uniforms

704
00:39:03,481 --> 00:39:06,320
to supply the millions of soldiers headed overseas.

705
00:39:09,220 --> 00:39:11,080
4 and 1 half million pairs of boots

706
00:39:11,180 --> 00:39:14,220
were produced here during the war.

707
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:17,120
The Valley's most important product was aluminum.

708
00:39:17,220 --> 00:39:21,140
It takes tremendous amounts of energy to refine aluminum.

709
00:39:21,240 --> 00:39:23,140
A single roll requires enough power

710
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:27,140
to run a typical house for 27 years.

711
00:39:27,240 --> 00:39:30,040
During World War II, aluminum meant air power.

712
00:39:30,140 --> 00:39:32,640
Training planes were built right in the Valley.

713
00:39:32,641 --> 00:39:35,140
in the valley, and huge shipments of aluminum

714
00:39:35,141 --> 00:39:38,680
were sent to factories elsewhere to build the super fortress,

715
00:39:38,681 --> 00:39:40,880
the B-29.

716
00:39:40,881 --> 00:39:46,680
A single B-29 bomber required 10 tons of aluminum.

717
00:39:46,681 --> 00:39:49,720
Without the rapid production of these mighty machines,

718
00:39:49,721 --> 00:39:55,000
America might not have ruled the skies of the Pacific.

719
00:39:55,001 --> 00:39:58,000
TVA made another key contribution to America's war

720
00:39:58,001 --> 00:40:02,640
machine, one that most people didn't know at the time.

721
00:40:02,641 --> 00:40:07,640
TVA provided power for a secret city of 50,000 people,

722
00:40:07,740 --> 00:40:11,720
a city with a mission so important that no one was

723
00:40:11,820 --> 00:40:14,260
allowed to talk about it.

724
00:40:14,360 --> 00:40:17,820
Oak Ridge would refine uranium to build the most destructive

725
00:40:17,920 --> 00:40:22,260
device ever invented, the atom bomb.

726
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:35,580
TVA became a crucial part of the war effort,

727
00:40:35,680 --> 00:40:38,440
and in doing so, ensured its own survival.

728
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:47,260
The Tennessee Valley Authority is the only New Deal

729
00:40:47,360 --> 00:40:49,720
construction agency to become a permanent part

730
00:40:49,820 --> 00:40:52,520
of the US government.

731
00:40:52,620 --> 00:40:56,760
TVA still exists to manage and maintain its ingenious network

732
00:40:56,860 --> 00:40:58,240
of multipurpose dams.

733
00:41:02,640 --> 00:41:06,180
These dams have worked flawlessly.

734
00:41:06,280 --> 00:41:08,780
Flooding is a thing of the past.

735
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:12,580
More than $2 billion in property damage has been avoided.

736
00:41:15,680 --> 00:41:21,220
The Tennessee River is now a vital cargo artery.

737
00:41:21,320 --> 00:41:25,900
Barges move efficiently along the 652-mile river,

738
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:28,740
ultimately connecting with the Mississippi

739
00:41:28,840 --> 00:41:32,640
and the world of international trade.

740
00:41:32,641 --> 00:41:38,640
The Tennessee Valley continues to be an industrial powerhouse.

741
00:41:38,641 --> 00:41:44,580
Cheap hydroelectricity had turned a valley of struggling farmers

742
00:41:44,581 --> 00:41:47,580
into one of America's greatest manufacturing hubs.

743
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:53,900
TVA has continued to face controversy,

744
00:41:53,901 --> 00:41:56,400
particularly from environmentalists.

745
00:41:56,401 --> 00:42:00,540
The agency built coal-fired power plants after World War II,

746
00:42:00,541 --> 00:42:03,780
World War II, which supported the destructive practice

747
00:42:03,880 --> 00:42:06,240
of strip mining.

748
00:42:06,340 --> 00:42:09,980
TVA also expanded into the contentious world of nuclear

749
00:42:10,080 --> 00:42:13,780
power, and one of its dams has pushed a tiny fish, the snail

750
00:42:13,880 --> 00:42:16,360
darter, into extinction.

751
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:18,860
Still, TVA's system of dams remains

752
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:21,800
an unequaled feat of civil engineering.

753
00:42:21,900 --> 00:42:25,700
Their supply of power has been steady and unwavering for

754
00:42:25,800 --> 00:42:26,700
decades.

755
00:42:26,701 --> 00:42:36,280
TVA's first generator, Unit 1 at Norris Dam, worked like a Swiss watch until its first

756
00:42:36,281 --> 00:42:41,920
overall 58 years after going online.

757
00:42:41,921 --> 00:42:47,220
The giant penstocks, where 31,000 gallons of water thunder every second to meet the electric

758
00:42:47,221 --> 00:42:52,220
turbines, have withstood the test of time.

759
00:42:52,221 --> 00:42:55,840
The men and women of TVA built their dams to last.

760
00:42:55,841 --> 00:42:58,600
legacy will live on forever.

761
00:42:58,601 --> 00:43:01,480
Well, I've often wondered what would have happened

762
00:43:01,481 --> 00:43:03,720
if TVA hadn't come in.

763
00:43:03,721 --> 00:43:06,660
We wouldn't have gotten as many benefits, I'm sure,

764
00:43:06,661 --> 00:43:08,480
as we're getting now.

765
00:43:08,481 --> 00:43:13,060
What they did was incredible, and a sense of fulfillment,

766
00:43:13,061 --> 00:43:18,360
and a sense of identity, and a sense of simply doing good.

767
00:43:18,361 --> 00:43:21,000
Nowadays, it's easy to think of the TVA

768
00:43:21,001 --> 00:43:23,000
as simply the federal government's largest

769
00:43:23,001 --> 00:43:24,900
public power company.

770
00:43:24,901 --> 00:43:28,140
But on the other hand, I think that legacy of the 1930s,

771
00:43:28,440 --> 00:43:32,040
that decentralization, that autonomy from Washington, D.C.,

772
00:43:32,041 --> 00:43:34,220
that identification with a particular region,

773
00:43:34,221 --> 00:43:38,320
that sense of multiple missions continues to this very day.

774
00:43:39,180 --> 00:43:43,800
The Valley of the Tennessee was once a place where people feared nature.

775
00:43:44,340 --> 00:43:48,300
But now its placid lakes are a place of beauty and serenity.

776
00:43:49,840 --> 00:43:52,500
TVA accomplished more than anyone thought possible.

777
00:43:52,501 --> 00:43:56,780
It not only conquered the ferocious spirit of an untamed river,

778
00:43:57,240 --> 00:44:00,500
it brought hope to a place that knew only despair.

